    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>She wants people to stop assuming she has Parkinson&apos;s disease and wants to know what the tremor is. [S]</item><item>She wants to keep working on stage, where the voice tremor shows on sustained notes. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Katharine Hepburn was born in Hartford, Connecticut, on May 12, 1907. [D]</item><item>By the 1980s Hepburn had a noticeable tremor that left her head constantly shaking; it was noticeable by the time of a 1979 television film and worsened later in her life. [D]</item><item>An obituary described her famous voice in her last years as quivery. [D]</item><item>Her tremor is identified as essential tremor by an encyclopedia article, a public radio report, and a physician&apos;s newspaper column, all of which note that it was widely mistaken for Parkinson&apos;s disease; an Associated Press obituary described tremors similar to Parkinson&apos;s disease. No public statement by a physician who examined her was found. [R]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

